		<link href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/viewtopic.php?p=38517#p38517"/>
		<title type="html"><![CDATA[Re: Armour - three-spoke dovetailing tiles - trispokedovetil]]></title>

		
		<content type="html" xml:base="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/viewtopic.php?p=38517#p38517"><![CDATA[
Stacking and joining those together forms a bilayer trispokedovetile, "C100+C121".<div class="inline-attachment"><dl class="file"><dt class="attach-image"><img src="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/download/file.php?id=1491" class="postimage" alt="C100C121.jpg" onclick="viewableArea(this);" /></dt></dl></div> <div class="inline-attachment"><dl class="file"><dt class="attach-image"><img src="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/download/file.php?id=1492" class="postimage" alt="C100+C121_3D.jpg" onclick="viewableArea(this);" /></dt></dl></div>Drawing the 2 layers semi-transparently we can see how the bilayer trispokedovetiles would interlock in the normal to the plane.<div class="inline-attachment"><dl class="file"><dt class="attach-image"><img src="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/download/file.php?id=1493" class="postimage" alt="100+121_crop.jpg" onclick="viewableArea(this);" /></dt></dl></div>2/3rds of the tiles can be slotted together, either the yellows and the blues or the yellows and the purples or the blues and the purples.<br> <br>However the final 1/3rd of the tiles would not simply slot in and would have be inserted by joining the two halves of the bilayer trispokedovetile in situ.<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="https://pybullet.org/Bullet/phpBB3/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=11936">Peter Dow</a> — Tue Nov 29, 2016 8:15 pm</p><hr />
]]></content>
